cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework for describing how a
  shared multidimensional real-resource world K, actor-specific access and
  usability views K_i, and actor-specific subjective evaluation and expectation
  states P_i condition actor-side processes and events A_i. K_i is not required
  to be a set-theoretic subset of K; it is a derived actor-relative access,
  usability, exercisability, or referenceability view of the shared resource
  world. For another actor a, P_j(a) denotes actor j's evaluation, belief,
  credit assessment, or expectation about a, rather than a state held by a.
  Delta K(S, tau) denotes a specification-indexed change descriptor for K under
  an observation and accounting specification S and interval tau. In general,
  Y_S(t)=M_S(K_t) and Delta K(S,tau)=delta_S(Y_S(t0),Y_S(t1)) in D_S, where the
  change operator need not be an arithmetic difference. Additive differences
  are a special case of a quantitative projection. Delta P_i is likewise a
  change descriptor or state transition and need not be an arithmetic
  difference. The same S-indexed change schema can be used across micro and
  macro scales even when value domains, coordinates, units, and aggregation
  rules differ. E_i[Delta K(S,tau)] denotes an actor-specific expected realized
  resource change where the relevant Delta K is represented on a quantitative
  projection for which an expectation is defined. Planned or chosen resource
  changes are distinct from such forecasts and may be represented by
  projection-local variables such as x_i. Preferences, desirability, credit
  assessments, beliefs, and related structures remain in P_i rather than being
  identified with the expectation itself. VFT does not fix a universal
  probability measure, expectation-formation equation, action-selection rule,
  market-equilibrium condition, or aggregation law. Economic equilibrium,
  budget constraints, accounting identities, surplus, decentralized adjustment,
  micro-macro linkage, and reputation- or credit-economy models are treated as
  domain-specific projection candidates requiring additional economic and
  measurement assumptions.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nuno929/vf-model"
